Falsifies `paging::sv39::a_table_entry_is_never_a_block`.

The defect: a pointer PTE is made readable. On RISC-V the leaf/pointer distinction is not a
type field; a PTE is a POINTER exactly when R, W and X are all zero, and a leaf otherwise. So
setting R on an intermediate entry, which reads as an obviously safe permission to grant (it is
only read, and the leaf below decides anyway), silently converts it into a megapage or gigapage
leaf whose physical address is the next table's. The privilege model makes this worse than it
looks on aarch64: the entry is `U`-clear, so the fault the hardware raises on a user access is
not the one anybody expects to debug.

Expected to fail: `assert!(!Sv39::is_block(Sv39::table_entry(pa)))`, for every `pa`, since
`is_block` here is `entry & (R | W | X) != 0`.

**Stated where a reader of the encoder will look**: `table_entry`'s own comment already says the
zero in R/W/X is what marks it a pointer. This patch is the proof that the comment is enforced
rather than merely written.

diff --git a/crates/paging/src/sv39.rs b/crates/paging/src/sv39.rs
--- a/crates/paging/src/sv39.rs
+++ b/crates/paging/src/sv39.rs
@@ -83,7 +83,7 @@ impl PageFormat for Sv39 {
 
     fn table_entry(pa: u64, _level: usize) -> u64 {
         // A pointer PTE: PPN plus V, and R=W=X=0 (that zero is what marks it a pointer, not a leaf).
-        (((pa >> 12) & PPN_MASK) << PPN_SHIFT) | V
+        (((pa >> 12) & PPN_MASK) << PPN_SHIFT) | V | R
     }
 
     fn leaf_entry(pa: u64, flags: Flags) -> u64 {
